seg-fac73c5cbbc2 = Every error message has a stable code, the message id in capitals (<c1/> is <c2/>), the same in every language. English command-line text is unchanged. In another language the line is <c3/>; with <c4/> a second line <c5/> follows in every language. The API gives <c6/> and <c7/> (<c8/>); MCP errors carry <c9/> as error data. Match on the code, never on the text.

# text
seg-7d731dc5f888 = How a run works in another language (the run-twice rule)

# text
# c1: result.json
# c2: report.json
# c3: report.html
seg-204df30d9ca2 = A machine file must not depend on the language. A run in another language therefore executes the scenario twice: once in English, whose <c1/>, <c2/> and CSV are the machine files, and once in the user's language, for the summary, the chart and <c3/>. English is one run, byte for byte what it was before.

# text
# c1: --lang de
seg-f68d19f253ca = Measured, native, release build, English then <c1/> (seconds): leo-navmsg-fit-interval-trade { "9.2" } -> { "17.9" }, cislunar-arc-recovery { "7.8" } -> { "14.1" }, lunar-station-coverage { "7.8" } -> { "15.0" }, clock-ensemble { "5.8" } -> { "11.6" }, leo-navmsg-model-comparison { "5.1" } -> { "10.5" }, leo-ppp-convergence { "2.8" } -> { "6.2" }; every other bundled scenario is under { "4" } s; all { "173" } bundled scenarios take { "64" } s in English. The cost is exactly twice for another language. WebAssembly was not measured (no wasm-bindgen here); at the browser factor of about { "4" } it is a minute for the slowest kind.

# text
# c1: run_human(toml, lang)
# c2: {summary, svg}
seg-cc148fdec7a2 = A page avoids the wait by showing the English result at once and calling <c1/> afterwards: that is the second run alone, it returns <c2/>, and the Studio can say "translating..." until it arrives.

# text
# c1: (message id, arguments)
# c2: t!
# c3: num()
# c4: t!
# c5: num
seg-cd0360ad624d = <b>{ "0.41" } item: one run.</b> Render both from a record of <c1/>. It was judged and not built: <c2/> results are inspected, measured and hashed after they are made (chart text widths, provenance hashes over output text), and <c3/> formats at the call site, so a record would put placeholder tokens into machine files and make their bytes depend on the replay. It needs token-returning <c4/>/<c5/>, a finaliser at every writer, and a byte-identity proof per writer.

